  "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=3$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,3].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(3)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{3}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3) & =4+{\\int }_{1}^{3}(2t+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4+{[{t}^{2}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =4+([{3}^{2}+\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}]-[{1}^{2}+\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}]) \\\\  & =4+([9+9]-[1+\\frac{1}{3}]) \\\\  & =4+(18-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =4+\\frac{50}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{62}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=3$ is $x=\\frac{62}{3}.$",
